Ld. Counsel for the accused submitted that the accused has been falsely implicated in the present matter; that the testimonies of the alleged eye- witnesses suffered from material contradictions and more so, they were not in sound state of mind at the time of incident being in drunken state as admitted by themselves, hence, cannot be relied upon; that as per the case of the prosecution, PW-6 Deepak called the PCR, however, during cross examination, this witness categorically denied to make such call; that prosecution deliberately did not bring on record as to how the accused suffered injury which points towards bias investigation and no blood of injured was found on the cloths of the accused, which fortifies his defence that accused was not present when the injured was hit by the bullet; that prosecution failed to prove that the firearm was used by the accused as no chance prints/finger prints from the alleged weapon of offence were collected by the investigating agency;
that the testimonies of the prosecution witnesses clearly indicate that no proper arrest or recovery was conducted in the manner alleged, which casts serious doubt on the story set up by the prosecution; that the failure to conduct proper ballistic examination, absence of GSR analysis, lack of photographic documentation, and reliance on incomplete and unsupported findings render the prosecution's scientific evidence wholly unreliable; that prosecution failed to bring on record the State vs Neeraj Kumar Chopra on 30 March, 2026 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/94916490/
22 intention or knowledge on the part of the accused to cause death, coupled with an overt act towards the commission of such offence.

It seems to us that in a murder case, the non-explanation of the injuries sustained by the accused at about the time of the occurrence or in the course of altercation is a very important circumstance from which the court can draw the following inferences:
"(1) that the prosecution has suppressed the genesis and the origin of the occurrence and has thus not presented the true version;
State vs Neeraj Kumar Chopra on 30 March, 2026 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/94916490/
23 (2) that the witnesses who have denied the presence of the injuries on the person of the accused are lying on a most material point and therefore their evidence is unreliable;
(3) that in case there is a defence version which explains the injuries on the person of the accused it is rendered probable so as to throw doubt on the prosecution case."

It was further observed that:
In a murder case, the non-explanation of the injuries sustained by the accused at about the time of the occurrence or in the course of altercation is a very important circumstance from which the Court can draw the following inferences :
(1) that the prosecution has suppressed the genesis and the origin of the occurrence and has thus not presented the true version;
(2) that the witnesses who have denied the presence of the injuries on the person of the accused are lying on a most material point and, therefore, their evidence is unreliable;
FIR No. 132/2019 PS - Vasant Kunj South (3) that in case there is a defence version which explains the injuries on the person of the accused assumes much greater importance where the evidence consists of interested or inimical witnesses or where the defence gives a version which competes in probability with that of the prosecution one."
